      <h2>The Korea-Japan split</h2>
      <p>
        The two countries play different roles: <strong>Korea is the lithium volume player</strong> — its trio
        supplies global automakers with NMC and increasingly LFP cells — while <strong>Japan pairs a lithium
        legacy (Panasonic)</strong> with the strongest lead-acid and industrial positions (GS Yuasa). The Korean
        makers also operate major plants in the US under the
        <a href="https://market.worldbatteryhub.com/usa-battery-market/">IRA build-out</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Our Read</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>My read:</strong> Korea and Japan are the battery industry's incumbents-plus — Korea scaled
        lithium first outside China, Japan holds the industrial depth — and their shared challenge is competing
        with Chinese cost on one side and US/EU local-content rules on the other.</p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> The Korean trio's US plants and Japan's premium positions are both answers to the
        same squeeze: Chinese price pressure plus policy pushing production into customer markets. Reading the two
        countries together shows the industry's middle tier — the incumbents defending share with footprint, not
        price.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>My editorial view, not investment advice.</em></p>
